  "id": "DrugBank_DB00741 - MONDO_4773",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Iridocyclitis (disease) through a mechanism involving the inhibition of inflammatory responses. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone is linked to Uveitis and Iridocyclitis via the intermediate of Fluorometholone, a corticosteroid with similar pharmacological properties. This relationship is supported by the presence of a direct indication from Hydrocortisone to Iridocyclitis, as well as indirect pathways through Uveitis and Idiopathic Uveitis. The shared mode of action—corticosteroid-mediated anti-inflammatory effects—underpins the potential for Hydrocortisone to be repurposed for Iridocyclitis. The evidence is consistent with the notion that corticosteroids, by suppressing immune-mediated inflammation, may alleviate the autoimmune and inflammatory processes underlying Iridocyclitis. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in modulating inflammatory responses, which is a well-documented mechanism in the treatment of uveitis and related ocular conditions.",
